{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-app starter-pack purchase appearing without the player tapping to buy and without the configured fingerprint or password confirmation. This is an unintended purchase caused by absent purchase confirmation, matching the Accidental Purchases indicator under Easy to Purchase. The reviewer only tentatively wonders whether it was a glitch, so the behavior is not definitively attributed to a technical fault that would suppress the label under R11.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "I didn't tap anything and my settings are set up to where I have to input my fingerprint or password to make a purchase and it did not do that.",
      "rule_applied": "Accidental Purchases: making it easy to accidentally spend money with no confirmation or ability to undo or refund the action.",
      "rationale": "The game allegedly completed a purchase without the player's tap or required authentication, describing an unintended purchase with no confirmation."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a recurring in-game advertisement interruption: an ad appears after every over, and the reviewer explicitly calls these ads forced. It also describes an accidental ad tap that closes the game and opens the advertisement, followed by restarting the app; this is attributed to bad app behavior or a UI issue, not a deliberate purchase or opt-out manipulation. The forced advertisement mechanic supports Advertisement, while the technical failure does not add another label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "After every over, an ad pops up.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; compelled to view advertisements",
      "rationale": "The game inserts recurring advertisements during play, and the review explicitly characterizes them as forced."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review distinguishes between the external claim that the game is ad free, which is marketing material and not coded under R13, and the actual in-game behavior: advertisements appear, and leaving one requires entering the Play Store. That makes the player compelled to encounter the ad rather than freely dismissing it. No reward for watching ads, payment mechanic, currency conversion, or other coded design behavior is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "to get out of the ad, you have to go into the play store",
      "rule_applied": "definition: compelled to view advertisements",
      "rationale": "The ad cannot be exited normally; the player must enter the Play Store to leave it, making exposure to the advertisement compulsory."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a daily streak mechanic that the game says was broken and then resets to the beginning, causing previously accumulated streak progress to disappear. The reviewer disputes the detection, but under R11 the possible error does not suppress the underlying deliberate streak-reset penalty. This is a daily-return incentive with a consequence for a broken or missed streak, so Daily Rewards applies. It does not clearly describe a general gameplay progression time gate, so Playing by Appointment is not added.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "said I broke my streak and though I did not, it sent me right back to the beginning",
      "rule_applied": "Daily Rewards definition and R11: daily streak reset remains codable even when the reviewer attributes the trigger to an error",
      "rationale": "The game uses a streak-based daily reward or return mechanic and resets accumulated progress when the streak is considered broken."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review primarily reports a technical malfunction: lag, unresponsive controls, and a sudden game stoppage are attributed to the game's network or technical failure, despite the reviewer saying their network works normally in other apps. The important design consequence is that the interrupted ranked match is recorded as a loss and deducts 30–50 points from ranked progress; under R11, the underlying match-forfeit penalty remains codable even though the malfunction triggered it. The mention of Duel Mode and ranked progress also describes competitive play. The golden network icons are only diagnostic indicators and do not describe UI misdirection, advertising, or monetization.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_MandatoryMarathon",
      "span": "game has suddenly stopped and says YOU HAVE LOST and then nearly -30 to -50 gaming points are lessed from my Ranked game progress",
      "rule_applied": "T_MandatoryMarathon definition and R11: when a crash, freeze, lag, or disconnection ends a match and the game's exit/forfeit rule deducts progress, code the underlying forfeiture rather than the technical fault itself.",
      "rationale": "An interrupted ranked session is forcibly ended as a loss and the player forfeits accumulated ranked points."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "Duel Mode game",
      "rule_applied": "S_Competition definition: the game makes players compete against each other in competition.",
      "rationale": "Duel Mode describes competitive match-based play."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes three mechanics. The long loading time is a technical performance complaint, not an in-game wait timer or an obscured mandatory download, so it does not receive a label. The complaint that the bonus lady rarely appears or matches describes an infrequent chance-based slot outcome, which is a variable reward delivery mechanic. The reviewer also says they are buying coins from the game; because coins are the acquired virtual currency, this supports Intermediate Currency, and in the slot-game context the purchased coins fund participation in chance-based play, supporting Gambling as well. The intention to check back occasionally and the hope that the rating changes the game do not describe FOMO, daily rewards, or another retention mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "buying coins from them",
      "rule_applied": "Intermediate Currency definition and currency-object boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes acquiring virtual coins through a purchase, indicating real money is being converted into in-game currency."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "bonus lady rarely comes out or even matches with anything on ww4. iv started playing a new game and buying coins from them",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling indicator for paid participation in a chance-based game; when purchased currency funds the chance mechanic, Gambling and Intermediate Currency both apply",
      "rationale": "The slot-game review links purchased coins with a chance-based bonus and matching outcome, indicating a paid stake in luck-based play."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "bonus lady rarely comes out or even matches with anything on ww4",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards indicator",
      "rationale": "The bonus feature's infrequent appearance and unpredictable matching describe chance-based reward delivery."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Cashman Casino \"bonus lady\" WW4",
  "search_result": "No reliable result established what \"bonus lady\" or \"ww4\" specifically names in Cashman Casino, so the labels rely on the review's plain description and the supplied slot-game context."
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes other players cheating, dissatisfaction with named events, repeated gold duplicates, a daily trade limit, and insufficient dice. The cheating accusation describes player behavior rather than a game mechanic that rewards harmful conduct. The event complaints are evaluations without a described mechanic. The duplicate-gold complaint implies repeated unwanted drops from a randomized reward pool, which matches Reward Mania. The trade and dice complaints state scarcity but do not explicitly show wasted currency, a purchase requirement, a wait timer, or a daily-login reward system. The final reference to 500 spaces is a review-length limit, not an in-game mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Way to many gold duplicates!",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards / Reward Mania boundary: assign where the span describes duplicate or unwanted drops from a randomized pool.",
      "rationale": "The complaint identifies repeated unwanted gold drops, implying a reward system that can repeatedly deliver duplicates rather than desired collection items."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a competitive board game against opponents and claims that outcomes are deliberately manipulated so the player loses regardless of skill. The statements that the game is \"meant to make you lose\" and that good play cannot change the result assert rigged or concealed outcome determination, which supports Illusion of Control. The reference to opponents receiving better counts than the reviewer also establishes player-versus-player competition. The mention of glitches is attributed to technical faults and does not support a dark-pattern label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "if you are meant to lose, no matter how good you play you will definitley lose",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs alleged rigging: assign when the review asserts that the game manipulates or determines outcomes regardless of player skill.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer claims that the game fixes losses so skill cannot affect the result, implying concealed or manipulated outcome determination."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the opponents get good counts and you get small counts",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The review describes the player competing against opponents in the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review mainly describes misleading out-of-game advertising: the player installed Gardenscapes expecting an escape game but found a Candy Crush-style match-3 game. Under R13, that advertising misrepresentation is outside the app's scope and does not receive a label. Inside the game, the reviewer describes earning only one star per completed game and having to play the match-3 game to earn stars for actions involving Austin. This is a repetitive gameplay task required for progression, supporting Grinding. The loss of points after failing a level is a consequence of failure, not a described appointment, purchase gate, or exit penalty.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Even for walking Austin you will have to earn a star by playing stupid game.",
      "rule_applied": "Grinding definition and boundary rule: repetitive or tedious activity required for progress.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer must repeatedly play the match-3 game to earn stars needed for in-game progression involving Austin."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes an in-game advertisement that cannot be closed and blocks continued play until the app is closed and restarted. This is a forced or unskippable ad, so Advertisement applies. Restarting the app is described as a workaround, but no lost progress or exit penalty is stated, so Mandatory Marathon does not apply. The blocked close control is not connected to a purchase or purchase opt-out, so UI Misdirection does not apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "Can't close the ads, therefore can't continue playing",
      "rule_applied": "Forced to watch ads or given rewards for watching ads",
      "rationale": "The ad cannot be dismissed and prevents the player from continuing the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game purchase of a large pack of coins, which implies real money was exchanged for virtual currency. It also explicitly says the purchase was accidental, indicating a low-friction or insufficiently protected purchase flow. The positive evaluation and successful refund do not suppress either mechanic. No gambling label is assigned because the review does not describe wagering money or purchasing chance-based rewards.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "accidentally bought a huge coin pack",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule: Purchases denominated in currency",
      "rationale": "Buying a coin pack describes real-world payment being converted into in-game currency."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "I messed up and accidentally bought a huge coin pack",
      "rule_applied": "Accidental Purchases",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly reports an unintended purchase, indicating the interface made spending easy to do accidentally."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review mentions a general microtransaction strategy, but does not describe a specific purchase mechanic, so that alone is not codable. It explicitly describes watching advertisements in exchange for in-game rewards. The failure to receive those rewards is attributed to a connection problem, so the technical failure itself is not coded; however, the underlying rewarded-ad mechanic is still present.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "when you watch the ads, it conveniently \"can't connect\" so you can't get rewards for the ads you watched",
      "rule_applied": "definition; R11",
      "rationale": "The span describes incentivized advertisement viewing through promised rewards, while the connection failure is a separate technical fault."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a base-building game with heroes, scouting, resource gathering, leveling, and construction timers. The explicit mechanic relevant to the codebook is that building progress is gated by waiting for building times, which is an in-game timer imposing a delay. The request for more mini games or periodic rewards is hypothetical and is not coded under R12. Resource gathering is mentioned but not described as repetitive or tedious enough to establish Grinding, and scouting other bases alone does not establish Competition.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "waiting for building times",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make players arbitrarily wait for something.",
      "rationale": "Construction timers delay progress and require the player to wait according to the game's schedule."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review is fragmented, but it describes a storage discrepancy: the game is presented as having a 1GB size while installing or running it fills the phone's storage. That implies the game's actual storage demand is substantially larger than the stated size, matching the hidden or fragmented download pattern. The comments about impressive graphics and fake pictures are evaluations or marketing claims, which are outside the in-game design scope and do not add labels.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "Tech_FragmentedDownloads",
      "span": "why you give the screen size 1GB my phone is storage full for this game",
      "rule_applied": "Small advertised App Store size vs. large mandatory in-game download",
      "rationale": "The reviewer contrasts a stated 1GB size with the game filling the phone's storage, implying that the true storage requirement is concealed until installation or play."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The game charges credits to enter levels. As level costs rise beyond the player's available earning rate, the player is blocked from playing and must either wait an hour or pay cash for more credits. The cash-to-credit conversion is explicitly described, and the paid credits function as a way to resume gameplay. The review does not describe repetitive tasks, a daily reward, or wasted leftover currency.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "leaves you waiting an hour to play again",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "An in-game wait timer prevents the player from continuing to play immediately."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "waiting an hour to play again or shell out cash for credits",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Skip / Free alternative",
      "rationale": "Paying for credits is presented as the alternative to waiting and allows the player to continue playing levels."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "shell out cash for credits",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes exchanging real-world cash for in-game credits."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a 7-day streak that can be lost, which implies a daily-return reward or streak system that tracks consecutive days and resets or forfeits progress when the streak breaks. The reviewer says the loss happened without an apparent cause, but does not explicitly attribute it to a bug, glitch, error, or technical fault, so the daily streak mechanic remains codable.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "lost my 7 day streak for no reason whatsoever",
      "rule_applied": "Daily Rewards definition; a streak with a consequence for losing it is an active daily-return incentive. Under R11, the unexplained loss is not explicitly attributed to a bug or technical fault.",
      "rationale": "The span describes a 7-day streak whose loss carries a penalty, indicating a daily reward or login-streak mechanic."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two possible mechanics. First, the game provides a limited number of attempts for daily missions, but it does not explicitly describe daily rewards, streaks, or a penalty for missing a day, so Daily Rewards is not supported. Second, new chances to play become available only after waiting nearly two minutes, which is an in-game timer restricting when the player can continue. The complaint about items not matching orders describes an outcome problem but does not clearly establish a coded dark pattern.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "it takes nearly 2 minutes for new chances to play",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make players arbitrarily wait for something, including lives or chances regenerating over time.",
      "rationale": "The player must wait for a timed replenishment of playable chances before continuing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
